undamentals favor Democrats in Minnesota, but the open seat, large anti-Flanagan outside spending, and Cook's downgrade to Lean D justify sitting below the combined baseline. Markets at 85-87% for Flanagan support a Lean D call with a mid-single-digit margin.", "watch": "A late surge of Republican outside money or further damage from the credential story could push this into true tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tina Smith (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's open Senate seat has become an unexpected battleground. With Tina Smith retiring, Lieutenant Governor Peggy Flanagan faces former sportscaster Michele Tafoya, and polls from mid-September through late October show Flanagan leading by about three to five points, with the most recent surveys at the narrower end. The state's modest Democratic lean and a favorable national environment help her, but the seat is not safely Democratic.\n\nWarning signs have accumulated: outside groups have spent heavily against Flanagan, Cook Political Report moved the race to Lean D citing candidate quality, and a controversy over her academic credentials drew national coverage. Tafoya has kept pace financially. Prediction markets still price Flanagan as a clear favorite, and most raters call the race Lean or Likely Democratic, but the margin is tight enough that a late shift could decide control implications.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8725, "margin": 5.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7776, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8131, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8217, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7867, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.784, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7719, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7956, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7935, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7758, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "